--TEST--
`Core\Serialize` refuses an object holding a host handle, naming the class it refused — `rule:classes/graph-copy`, `rule:security/isolate-values-cross-by-copy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2's third refusal, and the one with teeth: an open descriptor belongs to
// the context that opened it, and the handle carries a *key* into that
// context's own table. A copy would hand the other side a key into **its**
// table at the same index, so what crossed would read whatever that side has
// open there. The walk refuses it naming the class instead.
var $file = Core\IO::open('./data/note.txt', Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
try {
    Core\Serialize::encode($file);
    echo "encoded an open file\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
$file->close();

// The refusal is a mark on the class and never the shape of a slot: a key is a
// `uint` like every other number, and a value that is nothing but its own state
// crosses like any other. `Core\IO\Lines` holds the lines themselves.
Core\Serialize::encode(Core\IO::lines('./data/note.txt'));
echo "a walk holding its own lines crosses\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./data"]
--FILE data/note.txt--
one
two
--EXPECT--
Core\Serialize::encode(): a Core\IO\File holds a handle onto something this side of the boundary opened, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary — pass what identifies the resource and open it there
a walk holding its own lines crosses
